// PositionsResponse defines model for PositionsResponse.
type PositionsResponse struct {
	// AccountTotal What this account has made all in, across the positions above and its own charges beside them. It sits on the response for the same reasons realized_total does: it is an aggregate of exactly these rows, it arrives in the round trip the screen already makes, and the addition happens once on the server so every reader agrees on the answer. Present even for an account with no positions, where by_currency is empty and in_base is a plain zero.
	AccountTotal AccountTotal `json:"account_total"`

	// Cash The money the account holds, one entry per currency it has ever touched, ordered by currency code. It sits beside `positions` rather than among them because it is not a security and has no instrument — but it belongs on the same screen and in the same round trip: cash is a holding, and a reader comparing it with the papers beside it is doing the arithmetic this screen is for. A currency whose balance came to nought is still listed: an account that bought dollars and sold them all again HAS held dollars, and saying nothing about them is a different claim from saying the balance is zero.
	Cash []CashPosition `json:"cash"`

	// CostBasisRules Whether the cost basis behind these figures is the one the owner's country requires (see CostBasisRules); the same statement as SessionInfo.cost_basis_rules, carried with the figures it qualifies.
	CostBasisRules CostBasisRules `json:"cost_basis_rules"`
	Positions      []Position     `json:"positions"`

	// RealizedTotal What this account's closed deals have locked in, across all of the positions above. It sits on the response rather than being computed by the reader from `positions`, and rather than living on its own endpoint: it is an aggregate of exactly these rows, arrives with them in the one round trip the screen already makes, and — like cost_basis_rules — describes the whole list rather than any one row. Present even for an account with no positions, where by_currency is empty and in_base is a plain zero.
	RealizedTotal RealizedTotal `json:"realized_total"`
}

// PriceSource defines model for PriceSource.
type PriceSource string

// RealizedCurrencyTotal defines model for RealizedCurrencyTotal.
type RealizedCurrencyTotal struct {
	// Currency ISO-4217 of the positions this figure adds up — their own currency, not the base one
	Currency string `json:"currency"`

	// RealizedPnlMinor Sum of Position.realized_pnl_minor over the positions in `currency`, exact. Null when any of them has no such figure (one sold into another currency); `in_base` is unaffected.
	RealizedPnlMinor nullable.Nullable[int64] `json:"realized_pnl_minor"`
}

// RealizedGap Why an account's base-currency total could not be struck. `no_rate`: a rate the sum needs is not loaded yet; the figure appears once it is. A parcel with no purchase date does not withhold the total: such positions are left out and counted in `undated_positions`.
type RealizedGap string

// RealizedTotal What an account's closed deals have locked in, summed over its positions on the server, per position currency and in the base currency.
type RealizedTotal struct {
	// BaseCurrency The space's base currency (ISO-4217), same as Summary.base_currency: the currency of in_base, carried here so a client formatting the total need not go back to the session for it
	BaseCurrency string `json:"base_currency"`

	// ByCurrency One entry per currency the account's positions are denominated in, ordered by currency code, each one an exact sum within that currency. Amounts in different currencies are never added together: a single integer made of dollars and euros is denominated in nothing. Never has a gap — Position.realized_pnl_minor is published unconditionally, since an unrecorded purchase date costs no money and no shares — so a reader in this mode always has a complete answer. Empty exactly when the account has no positions at all, which is also how a client tells 'nothing to say here' apart from a genuine zero.
	ByCurrency []RealizedCurrencyTotal `json:"by_currency"`

	// InBase The same result in the base currency: the sum of the positions' figures — Position.in_base.realized_pnl_minor for positions in another currency, their own realized_pnl_minor for those in the base one — each already rounded per position, so the total agrees with the rows. Null when a rate the sum needs has not arrived (`in_base_gap: no_rate`). A position whose disposal released a parcel with no purchase day is left out and counted in `undated_positions`; a position whose `in_base` is absent for other reasons still contributes its realized result.
	InBase nullable.Nullable[int64] `json:"in_base"`

	// InBaseGap Why `in_base` is null, or null when there is a figure.
	InBaseGap nullable.Nullable[RealizedGap] `json:"in_base_gap"`

	// TaxWithheldByCurrency Tax the broker took from the account rather than from a payment, per currency, negative. A tax charged on a paper is already in its position's income; this one (in Russia, withheld on a withdrawal against the year's base) belongs to no position and is not split among them. Empty when there is none; not converted.
	TaxWithheldByCurrency []CurrencyAmount `json:"tax_withheld_by_currency"`

	// UndatedPositions How many positions were left out of `in_base` because a disposal released a parcel that HAS a cost but no purchase date — typically a transfer whose total basis was typed in by hand without dates. That cost cannot be converted at the rate of an unknown day, and never will be, so the position is left out rather than taking the whole figure down. A parcel with neither a date nor a cost is not counted here: it was sold as bought for nothing, needs no date, and is counted by unknown_cost_positions. The per-currency entries include every position in full. 0 when nothing was left out.
	UndatedPositions int `json:"undated_positions"`

	// UnknownCostPositions How many positions sold shares that arrived with no purchase price and are counted as bought for nothing (Position.has_unknown_cost): the whole of what they sold for is in this result as profit, in every currency, so it is HIGHER than the truth by whatever was really paid for them. 0 when every sale knew its cost.
	UnknownCostPositions int `json:"unknown_cost_positions"`
}

